# @better-websocket/client

A typed WebSocket client for the browser and React Native. Handles reconnection, keepalive, and event dispatch. Platform behavior is provided by adapters (`@better-websocket/client-web`, `@better-websocket/client-react-native`). Framework bindings (React, Vue, Svelte, Solid) are separate packages that subscribe to the core — they never control the connection.

## What this is

A wrapper around the native `WebSocket` API that adds the things every app ends up building:

- Automatic reconnection with exponential backoff and jitter
- Typed events with separate types for incoming, outgoing, channels, and requests — all in a single definition
- Request-response pattern over WebSocket with typed responses
- Channels for organizing events into groups over a single connection
- Acknowledgements to confirm message delivery (`send.withAck()`)
- Stale data sync to catch up after reconnecting
- Message deduplication to prevent processing the same message twice
- Keepalive pings with missed-pong detection so proxies don't kill idle connections
- Connection state and quality tracking
- Message buffering with TTL during disconnection
- Network-aware reconnection that pauses when offline
- Inactivity timeout to disconnect when the app/tab isn't visible
- Lazy connection that only opens when something needs it
- Middleware for intercepting incoming messages
- Search params injection on connect and reconnect
- Debug mode for development
- Message injection for testing
- Extension system for server-dependent features (channels, request-response, acks, replay, dedup) — all overridable via class extension
- Platform adapters for web and React Native — overridable via class extension
- Framework hooks that subscribe to the above without controlling it

## What this is NOT

- Not a Socket.IO replacement. No rooms or fallback transports. Channels are supported but they're client-side routing — not server-managed rooms with join/leave state like Socket.IO.
- Not a server. This is client-only. Your server speaks raw WebSocket — this talks to it.
- Not opinionated about your server's message format (but ships with a default).

---

## Quick start

The simplest possible setup — connect, listen, send:

```typescript
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';

const socket = createSocket<{
  incoming: {
    message: { text: string };
  };
}>('wss://example.com/ws', { adapter: new Adapter() });

socket.connect();

socket.on('message', (data) => {
  console.log(data.text);
});
```

That's a working WebSocket client with automatic reconnection, keepalive pings, and network awareness built in. The `Adapter` provides web platform defaults (visibility detection, online/offline). Everything below builds on top of this.

---

## Core API

### Defining your event types

Before creating a socket, define your types as a single interface. This keeps everything — incoming events, outgoing events, channels, and requests — in one place.

```typescript
// socket-types.ts

interface MySocket {
  // Events the server sends to the client
  incoming: {
    notification: { id: string; title: string; body: string };
    'unread-count': { count: number };
    'user-online': { userId: string; name: string };
  };

  // Events the client sends to the server
  outgoing: {
    'mark-read': { notificationId: string };
    'subscribe-user': { userId: string };
  };
}
```

Every key is optional. If your app only receives events and never sends, omit `outgoing`:

```typescript
interface MySocket {
  incoming: {
    notification: { id: string; title: string; body: string };
  };
}
```

When you omit a key, the corresponding API surface doesn't exist on the socket. Omitting `outgoing` means `socket.send()` is a type error — there's nothing to send. Omitting `channels` means `socket.channel()` is a type error. This is enforced by TypeScript at compile time.

The full shape of the definition, with all keys:

```typescript
interface MySocket {
  incoming: {
    // events the server sends to the client (top-level, not in a channel)
  };
  outgoing: {
    // events the client sends to the server (top-level, not in a channel)
  };
  channels: {
    // named channels, each with their own incoming/outgoing/requests
    // see the "Channels" section
  };
  requests: {
    // request-response pairs with typed responses (top-level, not in a channel)
    // see the "Request-response" section
  };
}
```

### Creating a socket

```typescript
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';

const socket = createSocket<MySocket>('wss://example.com/ws', {
  adapter: new Adapter(),
});
```

The generic parameter types everything: `socket.on()` autocompletes `incoming` events, `socket.send()` autocompletes `outgoing` events. If you pass the `Channels`, `RequestResponse`, or `Ack` extensions, `socket.channel()`, `socket.request()`, and `socket.send.withAck()` become available with full type inference. Your IDE knows exactly what's available based on your type definition and extensions.

### Connecting and disconnecting

```typescript
socket.connect();    // opens the WebSocket connection
socket.disconnect(); // closes it and tears down the socket
```

`connect()` is safe to call multiple times — if the socket is already open or connecting, it does nothing.

`disconnect()` is a full teardown:
- Closes the WebSocket connection
- Stops all reconnection attempts
- Clears the message buffer
- Rejects all pending `request()` and `send.withAck()` promises with a `DisconnectedError`
- Detaches platform listeners (visibility, network)
- Cancels any in-progress `searchParams` resolution
- Internally nulls out the WebSocket instance for garbage collection

After calling `disconnect()`, you can call `connect()` again to re-establish the connection. All `.on()` handlers remain registered — you don't need to set them up again. This makes `disconnect()` safe for use in auth flows: disconnect on logout, connect again on login, and your event handlers are still there.

If you're done with the socket entirely (e.g., a component unmounts, the user logs out permanently), just call `disconnect()` and stop referencing the socket. The garbage collector handles the rest — `disconnect()` already removed all external references (platform listeners) that would prevent collection.

### Listening for events

```typescript
const unsubscribe = socket.on('notification', (data) => {
  // data is typed as { id: string; title: string; body: string }
  console.log(data.title);
});

// later, stop listening
unsubscribe();
```

Only events from `incoming` are available here. Trying to listen for an `outgoing` event or a channel-specific event is a type error — these are separate namespaces.

### Sending messages

```typescript
socket.send('mark-read', { notificationId: '123' });
```

Only events from `outgoing` are available here. The payload is type-checked — passing `{ id: '123' }` instead of `{ notificationId: '123' }` is a type error.

An optional third parameter controls buffering behavior for this specific message:

```typescript
socket.send('typing', { channelId: '456' }, { ttl: 5_000 });
```

The `ttl` option sets the time-to-live for this message when buffered. It overrides the `bufferTTL` config for this specific message. See "Message TTL" for details.

This serializes the message using the `serialize` option (defaults to `JSON.stringify({ event, payload })`) and sends it over the WebSocket.

If the socket is not open and `bufferWhileDisconnected` is `false` (the default), the message is silently dropped. If `bufferWhileDisconnected` is `true`, it is queued and sent when the connection comes back (see "Message buffering").

**Note:** `send()` does not trigger a lazy connection. If the socket is in lazy mode and not yet connected, the message is either buffered or dropped depending on `bufferWhileDisconnected`. Only `on()`, `request()`, and `send.withAck()` trigger a lazy connection — see "Lazy connection" for details.

### Sending binary messages

For servers that accept binary data (Protocol Buffers, MessagePack, etc.), use `sendBinary()`:

```typescript
const encoded = GameInputProto.encode({ tick: 42, dx: 1, dy: 0 });
socket.sendBinary(encoded);
```

`sendBinary()` accepts `ArrayBuffer`, `ArrayBufferView` (e.g. `Uint8Array`), or `Blob`. It sends the data directly over the WebSocket without going through the `serialize` option. Unlike `send()`, it is not typed against `outgoing` — you are responsible for the binary encoding.

### Connection state

The socket is always in one of four states:

- `'closed'` — not connected, not trying to connect
- `'connecting'` — attempting to establish a connection
- `'open'` — connected and ready to send/receive
- `'reconnecting'` — connection dropped, waiting to retry

```typescript
socket.getState(); // 'connecting' | 'open' | 'closed' | 'reconnecting'
```

To react to state changes:

```typescript
const unsubscribe = socket.subscribe(() => {
  // Called every time the state changes (e.g. closed → connecting → open)
  // Read the new state with getState() — the callback itself receives no arguments
  console.log('state changed to:', socket.getState());
});

// Stop listening when you're done
unsubscribe();
```

This is a low-level API that the framework bindings wrap for you. If you're using React, Vue, Svelte, or Solid, use the `useSocketState` hook instead — it calls `subscribe` and `getState` internally and gives you a reactive value.

### Close events

When the underlying WebSocket closes — whether from a server-initiated close, a network drop, or a call to `disconnect()` — you can listen for the close event.

```typescript
const unsubscribe = socket.onClose((event) => {
  // event.code    — numeric close code (e.g. 1000, 4001)
  // event.reason  — optional string from the server (e.g. "token expired")
  // event.wasClean — true if the close completed properly

  if (event.code === 4001) {
    refreshAuthToken();
  }
});
```

**Important:** `onClose` fires on *every* WebSocket close, including closes that trigger automatic reconnection. If the connection drops and the client starts reconnecting, `onClose` fires first, then the state transitions to `'reconnecting'`. If you only want to react to terminal closes (no more retries), check the state inside your handler:

```typescript
socket.onClose((event) => {
  if (socket.getState() === 'closed') {
    // This is a terminal close — no reconnection will happen
  }
});
```

Common close codes:
- `1000` — normal closure (you called `disconnect()`)
- `1001` — server going away (restart, deploy)
- `1006` — abnormal closure (connection dropped unexpectedly)
- `4000-4999` — application-defined codes (your server can use these for specific reasons like auth expiry, rate limiting, etc.)

### Error events

```typescript
const unsubscribe = socket.onError((error) => {
  // called when the WebSocket hits a protocol error
  // NOT called on normal disconnection — that's a state change
  console.error('WebSocket error:', error);
});
```

### Selected protocol

If you passed `protocols` in the options, the server picks one during the connection handshake. You can check which one it selected:

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  protocols: ['graphql-transport-ws', 'graphql-ws'],
});

socket.connect();

// After the connection is open:
socket.protocol; // e.g. 'graphql-transport-ws' — whichever the server chose
                 // null if no protocols were requested or the connection isn't open
```

---

## The Endpoint interface

The socket object and channel objects share a common set of methods called the **Endpoint** interface:

- `on(event, handler)` — listen for incoming events
- `send(event, payload)` — send outgoing events (fire-and-forget)
- `send.withAck(event, payload)` — send and wait for server acknowledgement (requires `Ack` extension)
- `request(event, payload)` — send a request and wait for a typed response (requires `RequestResponse` extension)

Which methods are available depends on what you defined and which extensions you use. If a channel has no `outgoing`, its `send` doesn't exist. If the `RequestResponse` extension isn't included, `request()` doesn't exist. TypeScript enforces this — you only see what you defined and what your extensions provide.

The socket extends the Endpoint interface with connection-specific methods: `connect()`, `disconnect()`, `subscribe()`, `getState()`, `getQuality()`, `getLatency()`, `onClose()`, `onError()`, `onQualityChange()`, `use()` (middleware), `setDebug()`, `sendBinary()`, and the `protocol` property. Extensions can add more: `channel()` (from `Channels`), `request()` (from `RequestResponse`), `send.withAck()` (from `Ack`).

Channel objects are purely Endpoint — they delegate to the parent socket's connection internally and just route events to/from the right handlers.

This shared interface means utility functions can accept either:

```typescript
function logAllEvents(endpoint: Endpoint<SomeIncoming, any, any>) {
  // works with both socket and channel objects
}
```

---

## Request-response

Normally WebSocket is fire-and-forget: you send a message and there's no built-in way to get a reply. Request-response adds that. You send a message and get a Promise that resolves when the server replies to that specific message.

Under the hood, each request is tagged with a unique ID. The server includes that same ID in its response. The library matches the response to the right Promise.

Define your requests in the type definition with `payload` (what you send) and `response` (what you get back):

```typescript
interface MySocket {
  incoming: {
    notification: { id: string; title: string };
  };
  requests: {
    'get-balance': {
      payload: { userId: string };
      response: { balance: number; currency: string };
    };
    'get-profile': {
      payload: { userId: string };
      response: { name: string; email: string };
    };
  };
}
```

```typescript
// Send a request and wait for the typed response
const balance = await socket.request('get-balance', { userId: '123' });
// balance is typed as { balance: number; currency: string }

// With a timeout (default: 10 seconds)
const result = await socket.request('get-balance', { userId: '123' }, { timeout: 5_000 });
```

If the server doesn't respond within the timeout, the Promise rejects. If `disconnect()` is called while a request is pending, the Promise rejects with a `DisconnectedError`.

---

## Acknowledgements

Acknowledgements let you confirm the server received your message. Unlike request-response, you don't need data back — you just need "got it."

`send.withAck()` returns a Promise that resolves when the server confirms receipt. If no confirmation arrives within the timeout, the Promise rejects.

```typescript
await socket.send.withAck('chat-message', { text: 'hello', channelId: 'general' });
// Server confirmed it received the message
```

```typescript
// With a timeout (default: 10 seconds)
await socket.send.withAck('chat-message', { text: 'hello' }, { timeout: 5_000 });

// With both timeout and TTL
await socket.send.withAck('chat-message', { text: 'hello' }, { timeout: 5_000, ttl: 10_000 });
```

The `ttl` option sets how long this message survives in the buffer if the socket is disconnected. It overrides the `bufferTTL` config for this specific message. See "Message TTL" for details.

`send.withAck()` is typed against `outgoing`, just like `send()`. The difference is only in whether you wait for confirmation.

If `disconnect()` is called while an acknowledgement is pending, the Promise rejects with a `DisconnectedError`.

---

## Channels

Channels let you organize events into separate groups over a single WebSocket connection. Instead of opening separate connections for chat, notifications, and presence, you use one connection and tag messages with a channel name.

Channels are **client-side routing** — the library tags outgoing messages with a channel name and routes incoming messages to the right handler based on the channel tag. The server doesn't need to know about channels; it just needs to echo back the channel field that was sent. This is different from Socket.IO-style rooms, which are server-managed.

### Defining channel types

Each channel has its own `incoming`, `outgoing`, and `requests` — separate from the top-level events. This means the same event name (e.g., `'message'`) can have different types in different channels, and TypeScript will catch it if you use the wrong one.

```typescript
interface ChatAppSocket {
  // Top-level events (not in any channel)
  incoming: {
    'server-announcement': { text: string };
  };
  outgoing: {
    'set-status': { status: 'online' | 'away' | 'dnd' };
  };

  // Channel-specific events
  channels: {
    general: {
      incoming: {
        message: { text: string; from: string };
        typing: { userId: string };
      };
      outgoing: {
        'send-message': { text: string };
        'start-typing': {};
      };
      requests: {
        'get-history': {
          payload: { before: string };
          response: { messages: Array<{ text: string; from: string }> };
        };
      };
    };
    announcements: {
      incoming: {
        message: { text: string; from: string; pinned: boolean };
      };
    };
  };
}
```

TypeScript enforces the separation:
- `socket.on('message', ...)` — **type error**. `'message'` is a channel event, not a top-level event.
- `socket.on('server-announcement', ...)` — works, typed correctly.
- `socket.channel('general').on('message', ...)` — works, typed correctly.
- `socket.channel('general').on('server-announcement', ...)` — **type error**. Top-level event, not a channel event.
- `socket.channel('billing')` — **type error**. `'billing'` is not a defined channel.

### Using channels

```typescript
const general = socket.channel('general');
const announcements = socket.channel('announcements');

// Listen for events on a specific channel
general.on('message', (data) => {
  // data is typed as { text: string; from: string }
});

announcements.on('message', (data) => {
  // data is typed as { text: string; from: string; pinned: boolean }
});

// Send on a specific channel
general.send('send-message', { text: 'hello' });

// Request-response on a channel
const history = await general.request('get-history', { before: 'msg-100' });
// history is typed as { messages: Array<{ text: string; from: string }> }

// Acknowledgements on a channel
await general.send.withAck('send-message', { text: 'important message' });
```

Channel objects implement the same Endpoint interface as the socket — `on()`, `send()`, `send.withAck()`, and `request()` all work on channels with channel-specific types.

Events without a channel tag are delivered to handlers registered directly on the socket (the top-level events).

### Dynamic channels

If your app has many channels with the same event shape (e.g., a chat app where every room has the same events), use an index signature:

```typescript
interface ChatAppSocket {
  channels: {
    [roomName: string]: {
      incoming: {
        message: { text: string; from: string };
        typing: { userId: string };
      };
      outgoing: {
        'send-message': { text: string };
      };
    };
  };
}

// Any channel name is valid, all share the same types
const general = socket.channel('general');
const random = socket.channel('random');
const custom = socket.channel('my-custom-room');
```

---

## Stale data sync

When the connection drops and comes back, the client missed whatever happened during the gap. Stale data sync tells the server "I last saw message number 42, send me everything after that." The server replays the missed messages so the client catches up.

This is sometimes called "replay" or "catch-up."

The library automatically tracks sequence numbers on incoming messages. When the connection is re-established, it sends a replay request to the server with the last-seen sequence number. The server replays missed messages, and the client processes them through the normal event pipeline (middleware, deduplication, etc.).

**Replay is best-effort.** The client sends the replay request; if the server replays messages, they're processed normally through the full event pipeline. If the server ignores the request (e.g., it doesn't support replay), the client continues without catching up — no timeout, no error. Ordering of replayed messages is the server's responsibility — the library processes them in the order they arrive. Deduplication (if enabled) handles any overlap between messages received before the disconnect and messages the server replays.

**Note:** Stale data sync requires server support. The server must store recent messages and be able to replay them on request.

---

## Message deduplication

When the connection drops and the server replays missed messages, some messages might arrive twice — the client received them before the disconnect, and the server sends them again during replay. Deduplication prevents processing the same message twice.

The library keeps a set of recently-seen message IDs (bounded by a time window to prevent memory growth). If an incoming message has an ID that was already seen, it's silently dropped.

Messages that don't have an ID (i.e., the `Dedup` extension's `extractId` returns `null`) are always delivered — they are not subject to deduplication.

---

## Reconnection

When the connection drops unexpectedly (server restart, network issue, deploy), the client reconnects automatically using exponential backoff with jitter.

**Exponential backoff** means the delay between attempts doubles each time: 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s, 30s. This avoids overwhelming the server with rapid reconnection attempts.

**Jitter** adds a small random variation to each delay. Without jitter, if a server restarts and 10,000 clients all disconnect at the same time, they all try to reconnect at the exact same intervals and overwhelm the server again (this is called a "thundering herd"). Jitter spreads them out.

| Attempt | Base delay | With jitter (example) |
|---------|------------|----------------------|
| 1 | 1 second | 0.7 - 1.3 seconds |
| 2 | 2 seconds | 1.4 - 2.6 seconds |
| 3 | 4 seconds | 2.8 - 5.2 seconds |
| 4 | 8 seconds | 5.6 - 10.4 seconds |
| 5 | 16 seconds | 11.2 - 20.8 seconds |
| 6+ | 30 seconds (cap) | 21.0 - 30.0 seconds |

The backoff resets to 1 second after a successful connection.

Calling `disconnect()` stops reconnection. Calling `connect()` after a `disconnect()` starts fresh.

`maxReconnectAttempts` controls how many times the client retries before giving up. The default is `10`. After exhausting all attempts, the state transitions to `'closed'` and stays there until you call `connect()` again. Set to `Infinity` for unlimited reconnection attempts — useful for always-on dashboards and monitoring apps.

### Missed pong detection

The keepalive system sends a ping at a regular interval and expects a pong back. If the server stops responding to pings, the connection is effectively dead even though the WebSocket hasn't officially closed (a "half-open" connection).

The library tracks consecutive missed pongs:

| Missed pongs | Behavior |
|---|---|
| 0 | Normal — latency measured, quality updated |
| 1 | Connection quality degrades to `'poor'` as a warning |
| 2+ (configurable via `maxMissedPongs`) | WebSocket is force-closed, client enters `'reconnecting'` state |

This prevents the client from sitting on a dead connection indefinitely. The threshold is configurable via the `maxMissedPongs` option (default: `2`).

### Network-aware reconnection

When `networkAware` is `true` (the default) and the platform adapter provides `onNetworkChange`, the client pauses reconnection attempts when the device has no network. When the device comes back online, the client immediately attempts to reconnect with the backoff reset to 1 second.

On mobile browsers and React Native, this prevents wasted battery on reconnect loops when the user is in a tunnel, on a plane, or in an area with no signal.

**Clarification:** Going offline does not call `disconnect()`. The socket enters a paused state where reconnection attempts are suspended but the socket remains active — all `.on()` handlers stay registered, and the socket will automatically resume reconnection when the network returns. `disconnect()` is reserved for explicit, intentional stops (e.g., user logs out). Network drops are handled automatically without developer intervention.

### Inactivity timeout

When `inactiveTimeout` is set to a number greater than 0, the client tracks whether the app or tab is being used. If it has been inactive (hidden or backgrounded) for longer than the timeout, the client disconnects to save resources. When the user comes back, the client reconnects automatically.

This requires the platform adapter to provide `onVisibilityChange`. If the adapter doesn't implement it, the option is ignored.

- On the web, "inactive" means the browser tab is hidden (the user switched to another tab or minimized the browser).
- On React Native, "inactive" means the app is in the background.

```typescript
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';

const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  adapter: new Adapter(),
  inactiveTimeout: 60_000, // disconnect after 1 minute of inactivity
});
```

```typescript
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-react-native';

const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  adapter: new Adapter(),
  inactiveTimeout: 60_000,
});
```

```typescript
// Custom platform — extend the base adapter
import { PlatformAdapter } from '@better-websocket/client';

class MyAdapter extends PlatformAdapter {
  onVisibilityChange(callback: (state: 'active' | 'inactive') => void) {
    const listener = () => callback(someCustomCheck());
    someCustomEmitter.on('visibility', listener);
    return () => someCustomEmitter.off('visibility', listener);
  }
}

const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  adapter: new MyAdapter(),
  inactiveTimeout: 60_000,
});
```

### State transitions

```
closed ──connect()──> connecting ──success──> open
                          │                    │
                        fail              drop/error
                          │                    │
                          └──> reconnecting ◄──┘
                                    │
                              backoff + jitter
                                    │
                                    └──> connecting (retry)

open/reconnecting ──disconnect()──> closed
```

In plain English: calling `connect()` moves from `closed` to `connecting`. If the connection succeeds, it becomes `open`. If it fails, or if an open connection drops, it moves to `reconnecting` — where it waits (with backoff + jitter) and tries again. Calling `disconnect()` from any active state moves straight to `closed` and stops all retry attempts.

---

## Lazy connection

By default, the socket connects when you call `connect()`. With `lazy: true`, the socket waits until something actually needs the connection — the first `socket.on()` call, the first `socket.request()`, or the first `socket.send.withAck()`.

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  lazy: true,
});

// No connection yet — nothing is using the socket

socket.on('notification', (data) => { ... });
// NOW the connection opens — someone is listening
```

**What triggers a lazy connection:**
- `on()` — subscribing to events means you expect to receive data
- `request()` — you're waiting for a response, so you need a connection
- `send.withAck()` — you're waiting for confirmation, so you need a connection

**What does NOT trigger a lazy connection:**
- `send()` — fire-and-forget, no expectation of a response. If `bufferWhileDisconnected` is `true`, the message is buffered. If `false`, it's silently dropped.

When all listeners unsubscribe and there are no pending requests or acknowledgements, the socket disconnects after a configurable idle timeout:

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  lazy: true,
  lazyIdleTimeout: 10_000, // disconnect after 10 seconds with no listeners (default: 5000)
});
```

This saves server resources and battery on pages where the WebSocket is only occasionally needed.

---

## Connection quality

The library measures the round-trip time of ping/pong messages and exposes a connection quality indicator. This gives you a single reactive value to bind to in your UI — a colored dot, a status bar, etc.

```typescript
socket.getQuality(); // 'excellent' | 'good' | 'degraded' | 'poor' | 'disconnected'
socket.getLatency(); // last measured round-trip time in milliseconds, or null if unknown
```

Quality thresholds (configurable):

| Quality | Latency |
|---------|---------|
| `'excellent'` | < 100ms |
| `'good'` | 100ms - 300ms |
| `'degraded'` | 300ms - 1000ms |
| `'poor'` | > 1000ms or 1+ missed pong |
| `'disconnected'` | socket is not open |

Override the thresholds:

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  qualityThresholds: {
    excellent: 50,   // < 50ms
    good: 150,       // 50ms - 150ms
    degraded: 500,   // 150ms - 500ms
    // everything above 500ms = 'poor'
  },
});
```

To react to quality changes:

```typescript
const unsubscribe = socket.onQualityChange((quality, latency) => {
  console.log(`Quality: ${quality}, latency: ${latency}ms`);
});
```

**Note:** When `maxMissedPongs` consecutive pongs are missed (default: 2), the library goes beyond quality degradation — it force-closes the WebSocket and triggers reconnection. See "Missed pong detection" for details.

---

## Message buffering

When `bufferWhileDisconnected` is `true`, any calls to `socket.send()` while the socket is not open are queued in memory. When the connection is re-established, the queued messages are flushed in order.

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  bufferWhileDisconnected: true,
  maxBufferSize: 50,
});

// Connection drops...
socket.send('typing', { channelId: '456' }); // queued, not lost
socket.send('typing', { channelId: '456' }); // queued

// Connection comes back → both messages sent automatically
```

If the buffer exceeds `maxBufferSize`, the oldest messages are dropped first. This prevents memory issues if the connection is down for a long time and the app keeps sending.

The buffer is cleared when `disconnect()` is called — buffered messages are discarded because `disconnect()` is an intentional stop. If the developer wanted to keep them, they wouldn't be disconnecting.

### Message TTL (time to live)

Some messages become irrelevant if they aren't sent quickly. A typing indicator queued 30 seconds ago is meaningless by the time the connection comes back. TTL lets each type of message expire from the buffer after a set time.

`bufferTTL` accepts a number (applies to all events) or an object for granular control:

```typescript
// Simplest: single TTL for all buffered messages
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  bufferWhileDisconnected: true,
  bufferTTL: 10_000, // everything expires after 10 seconds
});
```

```typescript
// Per-event TTL for top-level events
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  bufferWhileDisconnected: true,
  bufferTTL: {
    '*': 30_000,              // default for all top-level events: 30 seconds
    typing: 5_000,            // typing indicators expire after 5 seconds
    'cursor-move': 2_000,     // cursor positions expire after 2 seconds
  },
});
```

#### Channel-aware TTL

Top-level events and channel events have separate namespaces. A top-level event `typing` and a channel event `typing` are different. Top-level `bufferTTL` handles top-level events. Channel TTL is configured on the `Channels` extension:

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  adapter: new Adapter(),
  extensions: [
    new Channels({
      ttl: {
        '*': {                          // default for all channels
          'start-typing': 5_000,
          'stop-typing': 5_000,
        },
        general: {                      // override for 'general' channel
          'start-typing': 3_000,
        },
      },
    }),
  ],
  bufferWhileDisconnected: true,
  bufferTTL: {
    typing: 5_000,                      // top-level 'typing' event
  },
});
```

`Channels.ttl` also accepts a number for a single TTL across all channel events:

```typescript
new Channels({ ttl: 3_000 })  // all channel events get 3s TTL
```

**Resolution order** (most specific wins):

1. Per-call `{ ttl }` on `send()` / `send.withAck()`
2. `Channels.ttl.<name>.<event>` — specific channel, specific event
3. `Channels.ttl.<name>` (number) — specific channel, all events
4. `Channels.ttl.*.<event>` — all channels, specific event
5. `Channels.ttl.*` (number) — all channels, all events
6. `Channels.ttl` (number) — all channel events
7. `bufferTTL.<event>` — top-level specific event
8. `bufferTTL.*` — global default for top-level
9. `bufferTTL` (number) — everything
10. No TTL — message stays in buffer until flushed or dropped by `maxBufferSize`

#### Per-call TTL override

Override the configured TTL for a specific message using the third parameter of `send()` or `send.withAck()`:

```typescript
socket.send('typing', { channelId: '123' }, { ttl: 5_000 });

await socket.send.withAck('message', { text: 'hi' }, { timeout: 5_000, ttl: 10_000 });

general.send('start-typing', {}, { ttl: 3_000 });
```

Per-call TTL takes highest priority — it overrides everything in the `bufferTTL` config.

Expired messages are silently removed from the buffer. They are never sent.

---

## Middleware

Middleware lets you intercept every incoming message before it reaches your event handlers. Each middleware receives the parsed message and can do one of three things:

```typescript
socket.use((message) => {
  // 1. Return nothing → message passes through (most common)
  console.log(message.event, message.payload);
});

socket.use((message) => {
  // 2. Return null → message is dropped, no handlers see it
  if (message.event === 'heartbeat') return null;
});

socket.use((message) => {
  // 3. Return a new message → replaces the original
  return { ...message, payload: { ...message.payload, receivedAt: Date.now() } };
});
```

Middleware runs in the order you add it. If any middleware returns `null`, the chain stops — no further middleware runs and no `.on()` handlers fire.

---

## Authentication

### Cookie-based auth (same-origin)

If your app uses cookie-based sessions (e.g. Better Auth, NextAuth, Lucia), you don't need to configure anything. The browser automatically sends all cookies for the target domain on the WebSocket upgrade request — just like it does for `fetch`. As long as the WebSocket URL is on the same domain as your app, the session cookie is included automatically.

```typescript
// The session cookie for app.example.com is sent automatically
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>('wss://app.example.com/ws/notifications');
```

Your server reads the cookie from the upgrade request headers and validates the session, exactly like it would for a normal HTTP request.

**Cross-origin note:** For cross-origin WebSocket connections (the socket URL is on a different domain than the page), cookies may be blocked by third-party cookie restrictions in Safari and Firefox. In this case, use `searchParams` with a token instead.

### Token-based auth (cross-origin or stateless)

Many WebSocket servers require auth tokens or other parameters on the connection URL. Since the browser's WebSocket API does not support custom HTTP headers (unlike `fetch`), the common pattern is to pass them as query parameters.

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  searchParams: () => new URLSearchParams({
    token: getAuthToken(),
  }),
});
// Connects to: wss://example.com/ws?token=eyJhbG...
```

The function is called on **every** connect and reconnect. This means if a token expires and gets refreshed between reconnection attempts, the new token is used automatically:

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  searchParams: async () => {
    let token = getStoredToken();
    if (isExpired(token)) {
      token = await refreshToken();
    }
    return new URLSearchParams({ token });
  },
});
```

**Race condition handling:** If `disconnect()` is called while an async `searchParams` is resolving (e.g., a token refresh is in-flight), the connection attempt is cancelled. The resolved token is discarded and no connection is opened. Each connection attempt is internally tracked — if the attempt is no longer valid by the time `searchParams` resolves, it's abandoned.

You can pass multiple parameters:

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  searchParams: () => new URLSearchParams({
    token: getAuthToken(),
    client: 'web',
    version: '2',
  }),
});
// Connects to: wss://example.com/ws?token=eyJhbG...&client=web&version=2
```

---

## Debug mode

Enable debug mode to log every internal event to the console:

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, { debug: true });
```

Or toggle at runtime:

```typescript
socket.setDebug(true);  // start logging
socket.setDebug(false); // stop logging
```

Debug output includes:
- State transitions (`closed → connecting → open`)
- Messages received (event name + payload)
- Messages sent (event name + payload)
- Reconnection attempts with delay and jitter
- Ping/pong cycles, latency, and missed pongs
- Connection quality changes
- Buffer flush on reconnect
- Expired buffer messages (TTL)
- Deduplicated messages (dropped)
- Network online/offline events
- Visibility changes (active/inactive)
- Channel routing
- Request-response matching
- Acknowledgement matching

---

## Binary messages

By default, the client only handles text (string) messages. If the WebSocket receives a binary message, it is ignored.

To handle incoming binary messages, provide both `binaryType` and `parseBinary`. These options are linked — you must provide both or neither. TypeScript enforces this.

`binaryType` tells the WebSocket what format to deliver binary data in. `parseBinary` receives data in that format:

```typescript
// ArrayBuffer mode (most common)
createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  binaryType: 'arraybuffer',
  parseBinary: (data: ArrayBuffer) => {
    // decode and return { event, payload }, or null to skip
  },
});

// Blob mode
createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  binaryType: 'blob',
  parseBinary: (data: Blob) => {
    // decode and return { event, payload }, or null to skip
  },
});
```

TypeScript enforces the link: if `binaryType` is `'arraybuffer'`, the `parseBinary` argument is typed as `ArrayBuffer`. If `binaryType` is `'blob'`, it's typed as `Blob`. Providing `parseBinary` without `binaryType` (or vice versa) is a type error.

To send binary messages, use `sendBinary()` (see "Sending binary messages" above).

---

## SSR safety

The client does not access `window`, `document`, `navigator`, or `WebSocket` at import time. All browser APIs are accessed lazily inside `connect()`. This means you can import and create a socket in a server-rendered module (Next.js, Nuxt, SvelteKit, TanStack Start) — it just won't do anything until `connect()` is called in the browser.

```typescript
// This is safe in SSR / Node.js / React Native — no crash
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>('wss://example.com/ws');

// Only call in the browser (or React Native)
socket.connect();
```

---

## Platform adapters

The core socket has no platform-specific code. It doesn't know about `document.visibilitychange`, `navigator.onLine`, or React Native's `AppState`. Platform awareness is provided by **adapter classes** that implement `onVisibilityChange` and `onNetworkChange`.

### Class hierarchy

`@better-websocket/client` exports `PlatformAdapter` — the base class with all methods as no-ops. Platform packages extend it:

| Package | Class | Visibility | Network |
|---|---|---|---|
| `@better-websocket/client` | `PlatformAdapter` | Disabled | Disabled |
| `@better-websocket/client-web` | `Adapter extends PlatformAdapter` | `document.visibilitychange` | `navigator.onLine` + `online`/`offline` events |
| `@better-websocket/client-react-native` | `Adapter extends PlatformAdapter` | `AppState` from `react-native` | `NetInfo` from `@react-native-community/netinfo` (optional) |

Framework packages re-export `Adapter` from their platform package, so you only install two packages:

```typescript
// Browser app — import Adapter from your framework package
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-react'; // re-exports web Adapter

// React Native app
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-react-native';

// Vanilla web (no framework)
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';

// No platform features
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
```

### PlatformAdapter base class

```typescript
class PlatformAdapter {
  onVisibilityChange?(callback: (state: 'active' | 'inactive') => void): () => void;
  onNetworkChange?(callback: (state: 'online' | 'offline') => void): () => void;
}
```

### Custom adapters

To customize platform behavior, extend the adapter class and override the method you need. No need to reimplement everything — you inherit the defaults.

```typescript
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-react';

class ElectronAdapter extends Adapter {
  override onVisibilityChange(callback: (state: 'active' | 'inactive') => void) {
    const win = require('electron').remote.getCurrentWindow();
    const handler = () => callback(win.isFocused() ? 'active' : 'inactive');
    win.on('focus', handler);
    win.on('blur', handler);
    return () => { win.off('focus', handler); win.off('blur', handler); };
  }
}

const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, { adapter: new ElectronAdapter() });
```

Use cases for custom adapters: Electron, Capacitor/Ionic, TV/embedded (Tizen, webOS, Android TV), Web Workers, Node.js test environments.

---

## Options

All options are optional. The defaults are designed for the most common case — a WebSocket that reconnects automatically and sends keepalive pings. You only need to set options for features you want to enable or behavior you want to change.

```typescript
const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  // ── Connection ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  // Whether to reconnect automatically when the connection drops.
  // Default: true. Set to false if you want manual control.
  reconnect: true,

  // Maximum number of reconnection attempts before giving up.
  // After exhausting all attempts, the state transitions to 'closed'
  // and stays there until you call connect() again.
  // Set to Infinity for unlimited attempts (useful for always-on dashboards).
  // Default: 10
  maxReconnectAttempts: 10,

  // Maximum delay between reconnection attempts (default: 30000ms / 30 seconds).
  // The client uses exponential backoff: 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, ... up to this cap.
  maxReconnectDelay: 30_000,

  // Don't connect until something needs the connection (first on(), request(),
  // or send.withAck()). Disconnect after lazyIdleTimeout with no listeners
  // or pending requests/acknowledgements.
  // Default: false
  lazy: false,

  // How long to wait with no listeners before disconnecting in lazy mode.
  // Default: 5000ms (5 seconds)
  lazyIdleTimeout: 5_000,

  // Protocols to pass to the WebSocket constructor (e.g. ['graphql-ws']).
  // Most apps don't need this.
  // Default: none
  protocols: string[],

  // ── Keepalive ──────────────────────────────────────────────

  // How often to send a keepalive ping (default: 30000ms / 30 seconds).
  // Set to 0 to disable.
  pingInterval: 30_000,

  // The string to send as a ping. Your server should auto-reply
  // with the pongMessage. Default: 'ping'
  pingMessage: 'ping',

  // The string the server replies with. The client ignores this
  // message instead of trying to parse it as an event.
  // Default: 'pong'
  pongMessage: 'pong',

  // How many consecutive missed pongs before force-closing the WebSocket
  // and entering the reconnecting state. Default: 2
  maxMissedPongs: 2,

  // ── Buffering ──────────────────────────────────────────────

  // Queue outgoing messages while disconnected and send them when
  // the connection comes back. Default: false (messages are dropped).
  bufferWhileDisconnected: false,

  // Maximum number of messages to buffer while disconnected.
  // Oldest messages are dropped when the buffer is full.
  // Only applies when bufferWhileDisconnected is true.
  // Default: 50
  maxBufferSize: 50,

  // Per-event TTL for buffered top-level messages. Accepts a number (all events)
  // or an object with per-event granularity. Channel TTL is configured on the
  // Channels extension via its `ttl` option. See "Message TTL" section for details.
  // Default: none (messages stay until flushed or dropped by maxBufferSize)
  bufferTTL: number | {
    '*'?: number;
    [event: string]: number | undefined;
  },

  // ── Platform & Network ─────────────────────────────────────

  // Platform adapter instance. Provides onVisibilityChange and
  // onNetworkChange implementations. To customize, extend the adapter
  // class and override methods. See "Platform adapters" section.
  // Default: none (platform features disabled)
  adapter: new Adapter(),

  // Pause reconnection when the device is offline. Resume immediately
  // when it comes back online with the backoff reset to 1 second.
  // Requires adapter.onNetworkChange to be implemented.
  // Default: true
  networkAware: true,

  // When the app or tab has been inactive for this many milliseconds,
  // disconnect the socket. Reconnect when the app becomes active.
  // Requires adapter.onVisibilityChange to be implemented.
  // Default: 0 (disabled).
  inactiveTimeout: 0,

  // ── Quality ────────────────────────────────────────────────

  // Override connection quality thresholds (in milliseconds of latency).
  // Default: { excellent: 100, good: 300, degraded: 1000 }
  qualityThresholds: { excellent: number; good: number; degraded: number },

  // ── Extensions ──────────────────────────────────────────────

  // Extensions that add server-dependent features. Each extension
  // is a class instance. Built-in extensions: Channels, RequestResponse,
  // Ack, Replay, Dedup. See "Extensions" section for details.
  // Extensions can add methods to the socket (e.g., channel(), request()).
  // TypeScript infers the available API from the extensions array.
  // Default: none
  extensions: [
    new Channels(),
    new RequestResponse(),
    new Ack(),
    new Replay(),
    new Dedup({ windowMs: 60_000 }),
  ],

  // ── Auth ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

  // A function that returns search params to append to the WebSocket
  // URL on every connect and reconnect. Can be async (e.g. to refresh tokens).
  // If disconnect() is called while an async searchParams is resolving,
  // the connection attempt is cancelled and the resolved value is discarded.
  // Default: none
  searchParams: () => URLSearchParams | Promise<URLSearchParams>,

  // ── Serialization ──────────────────────────────────────────

  // Custom message parser. Receives the raw string from the WebSocket
  // and must return { event, payload } or null to skip.
  // Default: JSON.parse expecting { event: string, payload: unknown }
  parse: (raw: string) => { event: string; payload: unknown } | null,

  // Custom binary message parser. Receives data in the format specified
  // by binaryType. Return { event, payload } or null to skip.
  // Must be provided together with binaryType — one without the other
  // is a type error.
  // Default: none (binary messages are ignored).
  parseBinary: (data: ArrayBuffer | Blob) => { event: string; payload: unknown } | null,

  // Controls the format of incoming binary data and the type of the
  // parseBinary argument. Must be provided together with parseBinary —
  // one without the other is a type error.
  // Default: none
  binaryType: 'arraybuffer' | 'blob',

  // Custom message serializer for socket.send(). Receives the event
  // name and payload, must return the string to send over the wire.
  // Default: JSON.stringify({ event, payload })
  serialize: (event: string, payload: unknown) => string,


  // ── Debug ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  // Log every internal event to the console.
  // Default: false. Toggle at runtime: socket.setDebug(true)
  debug: false,
});
```

---

## Default wire format

By default, the client expects incoming messages to be JSON with this shape:

```json
{ "event": "notification", "payload": { "id": "123", "title": "Hello" } }
```

And sends outgoing messages in the same shape:

```json
{ "event": "mark-read", "payload": { "notificationId": "123" } }
```

You can change this entirely with `parse` and `serialize`. See the real-world examples section for how different servers format messages and how to adapt.

---

## Real-world examples

### Notification system (Cloudflare Durable Objects)

A per-user Durable Object pushes notifications over WebSocket. The client listens for new notifications and updates the UI cache. The server uses the default `{ event, payload }` format. The client only receives — it never sends.

```typescript
interface Notification {
  id: string;
  type: string;
  title: string;
  body: string | null;
  readAt: Date | null;
}

interface NotificationSocket {
  incoming: {
    notification: Notification;
  };
}

import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';

const notificationsSocket = createSocket<NotificationSocket>(
  'wss://app.example.com/ws/notifications',
  { adapter: new Adapter() },
);
```

### Chat app (Hono on Cloudflare Workers)

A chat server sends messages, typing indicators, and presence updates. The client sends messages and typing events. Uses channels for rooms and request-response for chat history. The server uses `{ type, data }` instead of `{ event, payload }`.

```typescript
interface ChatMessage {
  from: string;
  text: string;
  timestamp: number;
}

interface TypingIndicator {
  userId: string;
}

interface PresenceUpdate {
  userId: string;
  status: 'online' | 'offline' | 'away';
}

interface ChatSocket {
  incoming: {
    presence: PresenceUpdate;
  };
  outgoing: {
    'join-channel': { channelId: string };
  };
  channels: {
    [roomName: string]: {
      incoming: {
        message: ChatMessage;
        typing: TypingIndicator;
      };
      outgoing: {
        'send-message': { text: string };
        'start-typing': {};
        'stop-typing': {};
      };
      requests: {
        'get-history': {
          payload: { before: string };
          response: { messages: ChatMessage[] };
        };
      };
    };
  };
}

import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';
import { Channels, Ack, RequestResponse } from '@better-websocket/client/extensions';

class ChatChannels extends Channels {
  extractChannel(_event: string, payload: unknown): string | null {
    const p = payload as Record<string, unknown>;
    return typeof p.channelId === 'string' ? p.channelId : null;
  }
  tagChannel(_event: string, payload: unknown, channel: string) {
    return { ...(payload as Record<string, unknown>), channelId: channel };
  }
}

const chatSocket = createSocket<ChatSocket>(
  'wss://chat.example.com/ws',
  {
    adapter: new Adapter(),
    extensions: [
      new ChatChannels({
        ttl: {
          '*': {
            'start-typing': 5_000,
            'stop-typing': 5_000,
          },
        },
      }),
      new RequestResponse(),
      new Ack(),
    ],
    parse(raw) {
      const msg = JSON.parse(raw);
      return { event: msg.type, payload: msg.data };
    },
    serialize(event, payload) {
      return JSON.stringify({ type: event, data: payload });
    },
    bufferWhileDisconnected: true,
    searchParams: () => new URLSearchParams({ token: getAuthToken() }),
  },
);

chatSocket.connect();

const general = chatSocket.channel('general');

general.on('message', (msg) => {
  appendToChat(msg);
});

chatSocket.on('presence', (update) => {
  updatePresenceIndicator(update.userId, update.status);
});

general.send('send-message', { text: 'hello!' });

const history = await general.request('get-history', { before: 'msg-100' });
```

### Stock ticker (Go server with gorilla/websocket)

A Go backend streams price updates as JSON arrays. The client only receives — it never sends events. Uses deduplication to prevent processing duplicate trades during replay.

```typescript
interface PriceUpdate {
  symbol: string;
  price: number;
  change: number;
}

interface TradeEvent {
  symbol: string;
  quantity: number;
  side: 'buy' | 'sell';
  price: number;
}

interface MarketSocket {
  incoming: {
    price: PriceUpdate;
    trade: TradeEvent;
  };
}

import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';
import { Dedup } from '@better-websocket/client/extensions';

class TradeDedup extends Dedup {
  override extractId(_event: string, payload: unknown): string | null {
    const p = payload as Record<string, unknown>;
    return typeof p.tradeId === 'string' ? p.tradeId : null;
  }
}

const marketSocket = createSocket<MarketSocket>(
  'wss://market.example.com/stream',
  {
    adapter: new Adapter(),
    extensions: [new TradeDedup()],
    parse(raw) {
      const [_id, event, payload] = JSON.parse(raw);
      return { event, payload };
    },
    searchParams: () => new URLSearchParams({ api_key: apiKey }),
    maxReconnectAttempts: Infinity,
  },
);
```

### Collaborative editor (Fastify + ws)

A document editing server sends operations and cursor positions. The client sends operations and selection changes. Uses request-response for saving, acknowledgements for operations, and replay for catching up after reconnect.

```typescript
interface DocumentOperation {
  docId: string;
  userId: string;
  ops: Array<{ insert?: string; delete?: number; retain?: number }>;
  version: number;
}

interface CursorPosition {
  userId: string;
  docId: string;
  range: { start: number; end: number };
  color: string;
}

interface EditorSocket {
  incoming: {
    'remote-op': DocumentOperation;
    'cursor-update': CursorPosition;
    'user-joined': { userId: string; name: string; color: string };
    'user-left': { userId: string };
  };
  outgoing: {
    'local-op': { docId: string; ops: DocumentOperation['ops']; version: number };
    'selection-change': { docId: string; range: { start: number; end: number } };
  };
  requests: {
    save: {
      payload: { docId: string };
      response: { success: boolean; version: number };
    };
  };
}

import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';
import { Replay, Ack, RequestResponse } from '@better-websocket/client/extensions';

class EditorReplay extends Replay {
  override extractSequence(_event: string, payload: unknown): number | null {
    const p = payload as Record<string, unknown>;
    return typeof p.version === 'number' ? p.version : null;
  }
  override buildReplayRequest(lastSequence: number) {
    return { event: 'sync', payload: { since: lastSequence } };
  }
}

class EditorAck extends Ack {
  override tagMessage(_event: string, payload: unknown, id: string) {
    return { ...(payload as Record<string, unknown>), ackId: id };
  }
  override extractId(event: string, payload: unknown): string | null {
    if (event !== 'op-ack') return null;
    const p = payload as Record<string, unknown>;
    return typeof p.ackId === 'string' ? p.ackId : null;
  }
}

class EditorRequestResponse extends RequestResponse {
  override tagRequest(_event: string, payload: unknown, id: string) {
    return { ...(payload as Record<string, unknown>), requestId: id };
  }
  override extractId(_event: string, payload: unknown): string | null {
    const p = payload as Record<string, unknown>;
    return typeof p.requestId === 'string' ? p.requestId : null;
  }
}

const editorSocket = createSocket<EditorSocket>(
  'wss://docs.example.com/ws',
  {
    adapter: new Adapter(),
    extensions: [
      new EditorReplay(),
      new EditorAck(),
      new EditorRequestResponse(),
    ],
    bufferWhileDisconnected: true,
    maxBufferSize: 200,
    bufferTTL: {
      'selection-change': 2_000,
    },
    inactiveTimeout: 300_000,
    searchParams: () => new URLSearchParams({ token: getSessionToken() }),
    debug: import.meta.env.DEV,
  },
);

// Request-response: save and wait for confirmation
const result = await editorSocket.request('save', { docId: 'doc-1' });
// result is typed as { success: boolean; version: number }

// Acknowledgement: send an operation and confirm delivery
await editorSocket.send.withAck('local-op', {
  docId: 'doc-1',
  ops: [{ insert: 'hello' }],
  version: 5,
});
```

### IoT dashboard (Python FastAPI)

A FastAPI backend streams sensor readings as JSON. The client sends configuration commands. The server uses `{ topic, value }` format. Uses unlimited reconnection since the dashboard should always be connected.

```typescript
interface TemperatureReading {
  deviceId: string;
  celsius: number;
  ts: number;
}

interface HumidityReading {
  deviceId: string;
  percent: number;
  ts: number;
}

interface DeviceAlert {
  deviceId: string;
  level: 'info' | 'warning' | 'critical';
  message: string;
}

interface SensorSocket {
  incoming: {
    'sensor.temperature': TemperatureReading;
    'sensor.humidity': HumidityReading;
    alert: DeviceAlert;
  };
  outgoing: {
    'set-threshold': { deviceId: string; metric: string; value: number };
    'subscribe-device': { deviceId: string };
    'unsubscribe-device': { deviceId: string };
  };
}

import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';

const sensorSocket = createSocket<SensorSocket>(
  'wss://iot.example.com/ws/dashboard',
  {
    adapter: new Adapter(),
    parse(raw) {
      const msg = JSON.parse(raw);
      return { event: msg.topic, payload: msg.value };
    },
    serialize(event, payload) {
      return JSON.stringify({ topic: event, value: payload });
    },
    searchParams: () => new URLSearchParams({ key: dashboardApiKey }),
    pingInterval: 15_000,
    maxReconnectAttempts: Infinity,
  },
);

sensorSocket.connect();

sensorSocket.on('sensor.temperature', (reading) => {
  updateGauge(reading.deviceId, reading.celsius);
});

sensorSocket.on('alert', (alert) => {
  if (alert.level === 'critical') showNotification(alert.message);
});

sensorSocket.send('subscribe-device', { deviceId: 'd1' });
```

### Multiplayer game (Bun native WebSocket)

A Bun game server sends game state updates. The client sends player inputs. The server uses Protocol Buffers (binary) for game state and JSON for waiting room events.

```typescript
interface GameState {
  tick: number;
  players: Array<{ id: string; x: number; y: number; health: number }>;
  projectiles: Array<{ id: string; x: number; y: number; dx: number; dy: number }>;
}

interface WaitingRoomUpdate {
  players: Array<{ id: string; name: string; ready: boolean }>;
  countdown: number | null;
}

interface GameSocket {
  incoming: {
    'game-state': GameState;
    'waiting-room-update': WaitingRoomUpdate;
    'player-eliminated': { playerId: string; by: string };
  };
  outgoing: {
    'player-input': { tick: number; dx: number; dy: number; shooting: boolean };
    'toggle-ready': {};
    chat: { message: string };
  };
}

import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';

const gameSocket = createSocket<GameSocket>(
  'wss://game.example.com/ws',
  {
    adapter: new Adapter(),
    parse(raw) {
      const msg = JSON.parse(raw);
      return { event: msg.event, payload: msg.payload };
    },
    binaryType: 'arraybuffer',
    parseBinary(data) {
      const decoded = GameStateProto.decode(new Uint8Array(data));
      return { event: 'game-state', payload: decoded };
    },
    pingInterval: 5_000,
    maxReconnectDelay: 5_000,
    inactiveTimeout: 30_000,
    bufferWhileDisconnected: true,
    bufferTTL: {
      'player-input': 1_000,
    },
  },
);
```

### React Native mobile app

A React Native app receives push-style notifications over WebSocket. Uses `@better-websocket/client-react-native` for automatic `AppState` and `NetInfo` integration.

```typescript
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-react-native';

interface MobileSocket {
  incoming: {
    notification: { id: string; title: string; body: string };
    'sync-required': { reason: string };
  };
}

const socket = createSocket<MobileSocket>(
  'wss://api.example.com/ws/mobile',
  {
    adapter: new Adapter(),
    inactiveTimeout: 120_000,
    searchParams: async () => {
      const token = await getSecureStoreToken();
      return new URLSearchParams({ token });
    },
  },
);
```

---

## Testing utilities (`@better-websocket/client/testing`)

A separate entry point for development and testing. Only import this in test files or behind `import.meta.env.DEV` checks — it is never needed in production.

### Message injection

Inject a fake message into the socket as if the server sent it. The message runs through middleware and dispatches to `.on()` handlers exactly like a real message. Works even when the socket is disconnected.

```typescript
import { inject } from '@better-websocket/client/testing';

inject(socket, 'notification', { id: '123', title: 'Test notification' });
```

Use cases:
- Manual testing: "What does my UI look like when a notification arrives?"
- Automated tests: trigger event handlers without a real server
- Load testing: "What happens when 50 messages arrive at once?"

```typescript
// In a test file
import { inject } from '@better-websocket/client/testing';
import { notificationsSocket } from './sockets';

test('notification handler updates the list', () => {
  inject(notificationsSocket, 'notification', {
    id: 'test-1',
    title: 'Test',
    body: 'Hello',
  });

  expect(getNotificationList()).toHaveLength(1);
});
```

To strip from production builds, use a dev-only import:

```typescript
if (import.meta.env.DEV) {
  const { inject } = await import('@better-websocket/client/testing');
  inject(socket, 'notification', testPayload);
}
```

Build tools (Vite, webpack, esbuild) eliminate the `import.meta.env.DEV` block in production, so the `/testing` entry point is never bundled.

---

## Framework bindings

Each framework has its own package. They are thin wrappers around the core's `subscribe()`, `getState()`, and `on()` methods. Each package re-exports `Adapter` from `@better-websocket/client-web` (or `@better-websocket/client-react-native` for RN) so you only install two packages.

### React (`@better-websocket/client-react`)

```typescript
import { useSocketState, useSocketEvent, useSocketQuality } from '@better-websocket/client-react';
import { notificationsSocket } from './sockets';

function NotificationBell() {
  const state = useSocketState(notificationsSocket);
  const quality = useSocketQuality(notificationsSocket);

  useSocketEvent(notificationsSocket, 'notification', (data) => {
    // data is typed as Notification
  });

  if (state === 'reconnecting') return <span>Reconnecting...</span>;
  if (quality === 'poor') return <span>Weak connection</span>;

  return <Bell />;
}
```

- `useSocketState(socket)` — returns the current `ConnectionState`. Uses `useSyncExternalStore` under the hood, so it's concurrent-mode safe.
- `useSocketEvent(socket, event, handler)` — subscribes to a typed event. The handler is stored in a ref internally, so you don't need to wrap it in `useCallback`. The subscription is managed by `useEffect` — it subscribes on mount and unsubscribes on unmount.
- `useSocketQuality(socket)` — returns the current connection quality (`'excellent'` | `'good'` | `'degraded'` | `'poor'` | `'disconnected'`). Re-renders when quality changes.

### React Native (`@better-websocket/client-react-native`)

Same hooks as React — `useSocketState`, `useSocketEvent`, and `useSocketQuality` — re-exported from `@better-websocket/client-react` internally. The difference is that this package exports its own `Adapter` that uses `AppState` and `NetInfo` instead of `document` and `navigator`.

```typescript
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter, useSocketState, useSocketEvent } from '@better-websocket/client-react-native';

interface MobileSocket {
  incoming: {
    notification: { id: string; title: string; body: string };
  };
}

const socket = createSocket<MobileSocket>('wss://api.example.com/ws', {
  adapter: new Adapter(),
  inactiveTimeout: 120_000,
});

function NotificationBadge() {
  const state = useSocketState(socket);

  useSocketEvent(socket, 'notification', (data) => {
    incrementBadgeCount();
  });

  if (state === 'reconnecting') return <ReconnectingIndicator />;

  return <Badge />;
}
```

### Vue (`@better-websocket/client-vue`)

```typescript
import { useSocketState, useSocketEvent, useSocketQuality } from '@better-websocket/client-vue';
import { chatSocket } from './sockets';

const state = useSocketState(chatSocket); // Ref<ConnectionState>
const quality = useSocketQuality(chatSocket); // Ref<ConnectionQuality>

useSocketEvent(chatSocket, 'message', (msg) => {
  messages.value.push(msg);
});
```

- `useSocketState(socket)` — returns a Vue `Ref` that updates reactively when the connection state changes. Uses `watch` with `onScopeDispose` for cleanup.
- `useSocketEvent(socket, event, handler)` — subscribes on setup, unsubscribes on scope dispose.
- `useSocketQuality(socket)` — returns a Vue `Ref` with the current connection quality.

### Svelte 5 (`@better-websocket/client-svelte`)

Uses Svelte 5 runes — no stores, no `onDestroy`.

```typescript
import { useSocketState, useSocketEvent, useSocketQuality } from '@better-websocket/client-svelte';
import { sensorSocket } from './sockets';

const state = useSocketState(sensorSocket); // () => ConnectionState (getter)
const quality = useSocketQuality(sensorSocket); // () => ConnectionQuality (getter)

useSocketEvent(sensorSocket, 'sensor.temperature', (reading) => {
  temperature = reading.celsius;
});
```

```svelte
<p>Connection: {state()}</p>
<p>Quality: {quality()}</p>
```

- `useSocketState(socket)` — returns a getter backed by `$state`. Reactively updates when the connection state changes. Cleanup is automatic via `$effect`.
- `useSocketEvent(socket, event, handler)` — subscribes via `$effect`, automatically unsubscribes when the component is destroyed. No manual cleanup needed.
- `useSocketQuality(socket)` — returns a getter backed by `$state` with the current connection quality.

### Solid (`@better-websocket/client-solid`)

```typescript
import { useSocketState, useSocketEvent, useSocketQuality } from '@better-websocket/client-solid';
import { gameSocket } from './sockets';

const state = useSocketState(gameSocket); // Accessor<ConnectionState>
const quality = useSocketQuality(gameSocket); // Accessor<ConnectionQuality>

useSocketEvent(gameSocket, 'game-state', (gameState) => {
  setPlayers(gameState.players);
});
```

- `useSocketState(socket)` — returns a Solid signal accessor that updates when the connection state changes. Uses `createSignal` and `onCleanup`.
- `useSocketEvent(socket, event, handler)` — subscribes on call, cleans up with `onCleanup`.
- `useSocketQuality(socket)` — returns a Solid signal accessor with the current connection quality.

---

## Server convention

If you control the server (or are building one), follow this convention and all features work out of the box with zero configuration.

### Message format

Every message is JSON with two fields:

```json
{ "event": "notification", "payload": { "id": "123", "title": "Hello" } }
```

- `event` — the event name (string)
- `payload` — the event data (any JSON value)

### Feature-specific fields

For features that need server cooperation, the library uses fields prefixed with `_` inside the payload:

| Feature | Field | Direction | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| Request-response | `_correlationId` | Both | Client adds a unique ID to the request. Server echoes it in the response. |
| Acknowledgements | `_ackId` | Both | Client adds a unique ID. Server sends back `{ event: "_ack", payload: { id: "..." } }`. |
| Channels | `_channel` | Both | Tags a message with a channel name. |
| Stale data sync | `_seq` | Server → client | A number that increases with each message. Used to track what the client has seen. |
| Stale data sync | `_replay` event | Client → server | Client sends `{ event: "_replay", payload: { since: 42 } }` to request missed messages. |
| Deduplication | `_messageId` | Server → client | A unique ID per message. Client uses it to detect duplicates. |

### Example: server sends a message with all convention fields

```json
{
  "event": "chat-message",
  "payload": {
    "text": "hello",
    "from": "alice",
    "_channel": "general",
    "_seq": 42,
    "_messageId": "msg-abc-123"
  }
}
```

### Example: client sends a request

```json
{
  "event": "get-balance",
  "payload": {
    "userId": "123",
    "_correlationId": "req-xyz-789"
  }
}
```

### Example: server acknowledges a message

```json
{
  "event": "_ack",
  "payload": {
    "id": "ack-456"
  }
}
```

If your server already follows this convention, you don't need to configure any adapters. Everything works automatically.

---

## Extensions

Features that need server cooperation — channels, request-response, acknowledgements, replay, deduplication — are implemented as **extensions**. The core socket doesn't hardcode these features; it discovers them from the `extensions` array.

Extensions are classes that hook into the message pipeline. Each extension can intercept incoming/outgoing messages and optionally add methods to the socket's API surface. TypeScript infers the available API from the extensions you pass — if you don't include `Channels`, `socket.channel()` doesn't exist at the type level.

### Extension base class

All extensions extend the `Extension` base class exported by `@better-websocket/client`:

```typescript
abstract class Extension<TApi = {}, TRequires = {}> {
  abstract id: string;

  onIncoming?(message: { event: string; payload: unknown }):
    { event: string; payload: unknown } | null;

  onOutgoing?(event: string, payload: unknown): unknown;

  onConnect?(): void;
  onReconnect?(): { event: string; payload: unknown } | null;
  onDisconnect?(): void;

  declare api: TApi;
}
```

- `TApi` — the methods this extension adds to the socket. Type-level only (`declare` — erased at compile time).
- `TRequires` — the properties this extension requires on the socket contract. Used for compile-time constraint checking — if the contract doesn't have the required properties, TypeScript errors when the extension is added to the `extensions` array.
- `onIncoming` — intercept incoming messages before dispatch. Return the message to pass it through, return a modified message to transform it, or return `null` to consume it (prevent dispatch to `.on()` handlers).
- `onOutgoing` — transform outgoing messages before serialization. Return the modified payload.
- `onConnect` — called when the WebSocket connection opens.
- `onReconnect` — called on reconnection. Return a message to send (e.g., a replay request) or `null`.
- `onDisconnect` — called when the WebSocket connection closes.

### Requires utility type

Extensions declare what they need from the socket contract using the `Requires` utility type:

```typescript
type Requires<R extends {
  incoming?: string[];
  outgoing?: string[];
  channels?: string[];
  requests?: string[];
}> =
  (R['incoming'] extends string[] ? { incoming: Record<R['incoming'][number], any> } : {}) &
  (R['outgoing'] extends string[] ? { outgoing: Record<R['outgoing'][number], any> } : {}) &
  (R['channels'] extends string[] ? { channels: Record<R['channels'][number], any> } : {}) &
  (R['requests'] extends string[] ? { requests: Record<R['requests'][number], any> } : {});
```

### Type inference

The socket contract type (`T`) flows from `createSocket<T>` into the extensions array automatically — extensions don't need manual generic parameters:

```typescript
function createSocket<T>(url: string, options: {
  extensions?: ValidExtensions<T>;
  // ...
}): MergeExtensions<T, typeof options.extensions>;
```

Internally, `createSocket` checks each extension's `TRequires` against `T` and merges all `TApi` types into the socket:

```typescript
type MergeExtensions<T, Exts extends readonly Extension[]> =
  BaseSocket<T> & UnionToIntersection<Exts[number]['api']>;
```

Each built-in extension declares its API and requirements:

```typescript
type ChannelsRequires = Requires<{ channels: [] }>;  // requires 'channels' key to exist

interface ChannelsAPI<T> {
  channel<K extends keyof T['channels']>(name: K): Endpoint<T['channels'][K]>;
}

class Channels<T extends ChannelsRequires> extends Extension<ChannelsAPI<T>, ChannelsRequires> { ... }
```

```typescript
type RequestResponseRequires = Requires<{ requests: [] }>;

interface RequestResponseAPI<T> {
  request<K extends keyof T['requests']>(
    event: K, payload: T['requests'][K]['payload'], options?: { timeout?: number }
  ): Promise<T['requests'][K]['response']>;
}

class RequestResponse<T extends RequestResponseRequires>
  extends Extension<RequestResponseAPI<T>, RequestResponseRequires> { ... }
```

```typescript
interface AckAPI {
  send: {
    withAck(event: string, payload: unknown, options?: { timeout?: number; ttl?: number }): Promise<void>;
  };
}

class Ack extends Extension<AckAPI> { ... }
```

`Replay` and `Dedup` don't add API methods and don't require specific contract properties — they only intercept the message pipeline.

**What TypeScript catches:**
- Contract has `channels` but `Channels` extension is missing → `socket.channel()` doesn't exist (type error if called)
- `Channels` extension is present but contract has no `channels` → type error at the `extensions` array
- Both directions are enforced at compile time

### Built-in extensions

All built-in extensions are exported from `@better-websocket/client/extensions`. Their default behavior follows the server convention described in "Server convention". To customize, extend the class and override specific methods.

```typescript
import { Channels, RequestResponse, Ack, Replay, Dedup } from '@better-websocket/client/extensions';
```

#### Channels

Enables `socket.channel()`. Routes incoming messages to the right channel handler and tags outgoing messages with a channel name.

```typescript
class Channels extends Extension {
  constructor(options?: {
    ttl?: number | {
      '*'?: number | Record<string, number>;
      [channel: string]: number | Record<string, number> | undefined;
    };
  });
  extractChannel(event: string, payload: unknown): string | null;
  tagChannel(event: string, payload: unknown, channel: string): unknown;
}
```

Default: uses `_channel` field in the payload. The `ttl` option controls buffer TTL for channel events (see "Message TTL" for resolution order).

Override example (server uses `room` instead of `_channel`):

```typescript
class MyChannels extends Channels {
  override extractChannel(_event: string, payload: unknown): string | null {
    const p = payload as Record<string, unknown>;
    return typeof p.room === 'string' ? p.room : null;
  }
  override tagChannel(_event: string, payload: unknown, channel: string) {
    return { ...(payload as Record<string, unknown>), room: channel };
  }
}
```

#### RequestResponse

Enables `socket.request()`. Tags outgoing requests with a correlation ID and matches incoming responses.

```typescript
class RequestResponse extends Extension {
  constructor(options?: { timeout?: number }); // default: 10_000
  tagRequest(event: string, payload: unknown, correlationId: string): unknown;
  extractId(event: string, payload: unknown): string | null;
}
```

Default: uses `_correlationId` field in the payload. The `timeout` option sets the default timeout for all requests (overridable per-call).

#### Ack

Enables `socket.send.withAck()`. Tags outgoing messages with an ack ID and detects acknowledgements.

```typescript
class Ack extends Extension {
  constructor(options?: { timeout?: number }); // default: 10_000
  tagMessage(event: string, payload: unknown, ackId: string): unknown;
  extractId(event: string, payload: unknown): string | null;
}
```

Default: uses `_ackId` field. Server responds with `{ event: "_ack", payload: { id: "..." } }`. The `timeout` option sets the default timeout for all acks (overridable per-call).

#### Replay

Tracks sequence numbers on incoming messages. On reconnection, sends a replay request to the server.

```typescript
class Replay extends Extension {
  extractSequence(event: string, payload: unknown): number | null;
  buildReplayRequest(lastSequence: number): { event: string; payload: unknown };
}
```

Default: uses `_seq` field for sequence numbers. Sends `{ event: "_replay", payload: { since: N } }` on reconnect.

#### Dedup

Drops duplicate incoming messages within a time window.

```typescript
class Dedup extends Extension {
  constructor(options?: { windowMs?: number }); // default: 60_000
  extractId(event: string, payload: unknown): string | null;
}
```

Default: uses `_messageId` field.

### Passing extensions to createSocket

```typescript
import { createSocket } from '@better-websocket/client';
import { Adapter } from '@better-websocket/client-web';
import { Channels, RequestResponse, Ack, Replay, Dedup } from '@better-websocket/client/extensions';

const socket = createSocket<MySocket>(url, {
  adapter: new Adapter(),
  extensions: [
    new Channels(),
    new RequestResponse(),
    new Ack(),
    new Replay(),
    new Dedup({ windowMs: 60_000 }),
  ],
});
```

Only include extensions you need. If your app doesn't use channels, omit `Channels` — `socket.channel()` won't exist (type error if you try to call it).

### Custom extensions

Build your own extensions by extending the `Extension` base class. Custom extensions can intercept the message pipeline and optionally add methods to the socket.

#### Simple extension (no contract requirements)

```typescript
import { Extension } from '@better-websocket/client';

class Metrics extends Extension {
  id = 'metrics';
  private counts = new Map<string, number>();

  onIncoming(message: { event: string; payload: unknown }) {
    this.counts.set(message.event, (this.counts.get(message.event) ?? 0) + 1);
    return message;
  }

  getCount(event: string): number {
    return this.counts.get(event) ?? 0;
  }
}
```

#### Extension with contract requirements

Use `Requires` to enforce that the socket contract has specific events. TypeScript errors if the contract doesn't satisfy the requirements.

```typescript
import { Extension, type Requires } from '@better-websocket/client';

type PresenceRequires = Requires<{
  incoming: ['user-joined', 'user-left'];
  outgoing: ['announce-presence'];
}>;

interface PresenceAPI {
  getOnlineUsers(): string[];
}

class Presence extends Extension<PresenceAPI, PresenceRequires> {
  id = 'presence';
  private online: string[] = [];

  onIncoming(message: { event: string; payload: unknown }) {
    if (message.event === 'user-joined') {
      this.online.push((message.payload as { userId: string }).userId);
    }
    return message;
  }

  getOnlineUsers(): string[] {
    return this.online;
  }
}
```

If the socket contract is missing `user-joined` or `announce-presence`, adding `new Presence()` to the extensions array is a type error — TypeScript tells you exactly which events are missing.

Real-world extension ideas: metrics/analytics, end-to-end encryption, rate limiting, message logging, compression, presence, schema validation, message batching, priority queues.

---

## Package structure

The library is a monorepo with separate packages. Each package has its own build and can be installed independently.

```
better-websocket/
├── client/
│   ├── core/                 # @better-websocket/client
│   │   └── src/
│   │       ├── socket.ts         # Socket class, createSocket
│   │       ├── endpoint.ts       # Shared Endpoint interface
│   │       ├── middleware.ts     # Middleware chain
│   │       ├── quality.ts       # Connection quality tracking
│   │       ├── extension.ts     # Extension base class
│   │       ├── types.ts         # ConnectionState, SocketOptions, etc.
│   │       ├── testing/
│   │       │   └── inject.ts    # inject()
│   │       └── extensions/
│   │           ├── channels.ts      # Channels extension
│   │           ├── request-response.ts  # RequestResponse extension
│   │           ├── ack.ts           # Ack extension
│   │           ├── replay.ts        # Replay extension
│   │           └── dedup.ts         # Dedup extension
│   │
│   ├── web/                  # @better-websocket/client-web
│   │   └── src/
│   │       └── adapter.ts       # Adapter extends PlatformAdapter
│   │
│   ├── react-native/         # @better-websocket/client-react-native
│   │   └── src/
│   │       ├── adapter.ts       # Adapter extends PlatformAdapter
│   │       └── index.ts         # Re-exports hooks from client-react
│   │
│   ├── react/                # @better-websocket/client-react
│   │   └── src/
│   │       ├── hooks.ts         # useSocketState, useSocketEvent, useSocketQuality
│   │       └── index.ts         # Re-exports Adapter from client-web
│   │
│   ├── vue/                  # @better-websocket/client-vue
│   │   └── src/
│   │       ├── composables.ts   # useSocketState, useSocketEvent, useSocketQuality
│   │       └── index.ts         # Re-exports Adapter from client-web
│   │
│   ├── svelte/               # @better-websocket/client-svelte
│   │   └── src/
│   │       ├── runes.svelte.ts  # useSocketState, useSocketEvent, useSocketQuality
│   │       └── index.ts         # Re-exports Adapter from client-web
│   │
│   └── solid/                # @better-websocket/client-solid
│       └── src/
│           ├── primitives.ts    # useSocketState, useSocketEvent, useSocketQuality
│           └── index.ts         # Re-exports Adapter from client-web
│
└── server/                   # @better-websocket/server
    └── src/
```

### Exports map (`@better-websocket/client`)

```json
{
  "exports": {
    ".": "./dist/index.js",
    "./extensions": "./dist/extensions/index.js",
    "./testing": "./dist/testing/index.js"
  }
}
```

### Peer dependencies

| Package | Peer dependencies |
|---|---|
| `@better-websocket/client` | none |
| `@better-websocket/client-web` | none (uses browser globals lazily) |
| `@better-websocket/client-react` | `react >= 18` |
| `@better-websocket/client-react-native` | `react >= 18`, `react-native`. Optional: `@react-native-community/netinfo` |
| `@better-websocket/client-vue` | `vue >= 3.3` |
| `@better-websocket/client-svelte` | `svelte >= 5` |
| `@better-websocket/client-solid` | `solid-js >= 1.0` |

---

## Testing strategy

### Unit tests (core)

**Connection lifecycle:**
- Connect / disconnect lifecycle
- Multiple calls to `connect()` are idempotent
- `disconnect()` stops reconnection, clears buffer, rejects pending `request()` and `send.withAck()` promises with `DisconnectedError`, detaches platform listeners, cancels in-progress `searchParams` resolution
- `connect()` after `disconnect()` re-establishes the connection with handlers intact
- SSR safety (no crash when `WebSocket` global is missing)
- State transitions match the state diagram
- Internal WebSocket instance is nulled on disconnect for GC

**Reconnection:**
- Exponential backoff with correct delays (mock timers)
- Jitter adds randomness within expected range
- `maxReconnectAttempts` exhaustion transitions to `closed`
- `maxReconnectAttempts: Infinity` never stops retrying
- Backoff resets after successful connection

**Missed pong detection:**
- 1 missed pong degrades quality to `'poor'`
- 2 missed pongs (default `maxMissedPongs`) force-closes WebSocket and enters `'reconnecting'`
- Custom `maxMissedPongs` threshold is respected
- Pong receipt resets the missed counter

**Network awareness:**
- Pauses reconnect when offline, resumes on online (via mock `onNetworkChange`)
- Backoff resets to 1 second on online
- Going offline does NOT call `disconnect()` — socket remains active with handlers intact

**Inactivity timeout:**
- Disconnects after timeout when inactive (via mock `onVisibilityChange`)
- Reconnects when active
- Timer resets on each visibility change

**Lazy connection:**
- Does not connect until first `on()`, `request()`, or `send.withAck()`
- `send()` does NOT trigger lazy connection
- Disconnects after `lazyIdleTimeout` with no listeners or pending requests/acknowledgements
- Re-connects when a new listener is added after idle disconnect

**Events:**
- Event dispatch and unsubscribe
- Top-level `on()` only receives `incoming` events — channel events are not dispatched to top-level handlers
- Channel `on()` only receives that channel's events — top-level events are not dispatched to channel handlers
- Events with no listeners are silently ignored

**Serialization:**
- Custom `parse` / `serialize`
- Ping/pong filtering (pong messages are not parsed as events)

**Binary messages:**
- Incoming binary via `parseBinary` with `binaryType: 'arraybuffer'`
- Incoming binary via `parseBinary` with `binaryType: 'blob'`
- Outgoing binary via `sendBinary()`
- Providing `parseBinary` without `binaryType` is a type error
- Providing `binaryType` without `parseBinary` is a type error

**Close and error events:**
- `onClose` fires on every WebSocket close, including closes that trigger reconnection
- `onClose` fires before state transitions to `'reconnecting'`
- `onError` fires on protocol errors
- `socket.protocol` returns server-selected subprotocol or null

**Buffering:**
- Messages queued during disconnect, flushed on reconnect in order
- Oldest messages dropped when `maxBufferSize` exceeded
- Buffer cleared on `disconnect()`

**Buffer TTL:**
- Messages with expired TTL are removed before flush
- Messages without TTL are unaffected
- TTL timer starts when message enters the buffer
- `bufferTTL` as number applies to all top-level events
- `bufferTTL.*` applies as default for top-level events
- `Channels.ttl` as number applies to all channel events
- `Channels.ttl.*` applies as default for all channel events
- `Channels.ttl.<name>` overrides for specific channel
- Resolution order is respected (most specific wins)
- Per-call `{ ttl }` on `send()` overrides config
- Per-call `{ ttl }` on `send.withAck()` overrides config

**Middleware:**
- Runs in order
- No return → message continues
- Return null → chain stops, message dropped
- Return modified message → replacement continues through chain

**Connection quality:**
- Quality derived from ping latency
- Correct quality tier based on thresholds
- `'disconnected'` when socket is not open
- `onQualityChange` fires when quality tier changes
- Custom thresholds override defaults
- `getLatency()` returns last measured round-trip time

**Request-response:**
- `request()` resolves with typed response when matching response arrives
- `request()` rejects on timeout
- `request()` rejects with `DisconnectedError` when `disconnect()` is called
- Multiple concurrent requests resolve independently
- Uses correlation ID from RequestResponse extension to match
- Channel-level `request()` works with channel-specific request types

**Acknowledgements:**
- `send.withAck()` resolves when ack arrives
- `send.withAck()` rejects on timeout
- `send.withAck()` rejects with `DisconnectedError` when `disconnect()` is called
- Uses ack ID from Ack extension to match
- Channel-level `send.withAck()` works with channel-specific outgoing types

**Channels:**
- Channel-specific `on()` only receives messages for that channel
- Channel-specific `send()` tags messages with channel name
- Channel-specific `request()` tags requests with channel name
- Channel-specific `send.withAck()` tags messages with channel name
- Top-level `on()` receives messages without a channel tag
- Top-level `on()` does NOT receive channel-tagged messages
- Uses Channels extension to extract/tag
- Channel objects implement the Endpoint interface

**Stale data sync:**
- Tracks sequence numbers from incoming messages via Replay extension
- Sends replay request on reconnect with last-seen sequence
- Replayed messages pass through normal pipeline (middleware, dedup, etc.)
- If server ignores replay request, client continues normally (no timeout, no error)

**Deduplication:**
- Drops messages with previously seen IDs
- Dedup window expires after configured `windowMs`
- Messages without IDs (extension returns `null`) are always delivered

**Search params:**
- Called on connect and reconnect
- Appended to URL
- Async search params resolved before connecting
- `disconnect()` during async resolution cancels the connection attempt

**Debug mode:**
- Logs state transitions, messages sent/received, reconnects, quality changes, missed pongs, dedup drops, buffer TTL expirations, channel routing

### Platform adapter tests

- Web Adapter: `onVisibilityChange` uses `document.visibilitychange`
- Web Adapter: `onNetworkChange` uses `navigator.onLine` + events
- RN Adapter: `onVisibilityChange` uses `AppState`
- RN Adapter: `onNetworkChange` uses `NetInfo` when available
- RN Adapter: `onNetworkChange` gracefully disabled when `@react-native-community/netinfo` is not installed
- Custom adapter class extension overrides specific methods
- Missing platform globals (SSR) disables features gracefully
- Platform listeners are detached on `disconnect()`

### Extension system tests

- Extensions receive `onIncoming` calls for every incoming message
- Extensions receive `onOutgoing` calls for every outgoing message
- Extension `onIncoming` returning `null` consumes the message
- Extension `onIncoming` returning modified message replaces the original
- Extensions run in array order
- Extension `onConnect` called when WebSocket opens
- Extension `onReconnect` called on reconnection, returned message is sent
- Extension `onDisconnect` called when WebSocket closes
- Extensions that declare API surface add methods to the socket type
- Omitting an extension removes its API from the socket type (compile-time check)
- Custom extension class extending built-in extension inherits default behavior
- Custom extension class can override specific methods

### Integration tests (framework bindings)

- React: `useSocketState` renders correct state, re-renders on change. `useSocketEvent` handler fires, cleanup on unmount. `useSocketQuality` re-renders on quality change. Works with `useSyncExternalStore` concurrent features.
- React Native: same as React, verify platform defaults are applied
- Vue: `useSocketState` ref updates reactively. `useSocketEvent` cleans up on scope dispose. `useSocketQuality` ref updates on quality change.
- Svelte: `useSocketState` rune state updates. `useSocketEvent` handler fires, cleanup on destroy. `useSocketQuality` rune updates.
- Solid: `useSocketState` signal updates. `useSocketEvent` cleanup fires via `onCleanup`. `useSocketQuality` signal updates.

### Testing utilities tests

- `inject()` dispatches message through middleware to event handlers
- `inject()` works when socket is disconnected
- `inject()` respects middleware (null return drops injected message)
- Injected messages are typed against `incoming`

### E2E test

- Spin up a WebSocket server
- Connect, send/receive typed messages
- Kill server, verify reconnection with backoff and jitter
- Verify missed pong detection triggers reconnection
- Verify search params on reconnect
- Verify async search params cancellation on disconnect
- Verify buffer flush after reconnect
- Verify buffer TTL expiration
- Verify stale data sync replay
- Verify deduplication during replay
- Verify request-response round-trip with typed responses
- Verify acknowledgement round-trip via `send.withAck()`
- Verify channel routing with separate event namespaces
- Verify channel-level request-response
- Verify channel-level acknowledgements
